The path profile describes the prescribed approach corridor; the outcome
determines the final offset/contact. Clean passage is credited by interpolating
the exact plane crossing and verifying aperture clearance. Rim contact requires
the hoop as the intended contact. Near-miss requires bounded clearance with no
aperture crossing or rim contact. Floor-bounce recipes require ordered floor
contact before the requested hoop outcome.

### 6.7 Bounded temporal and multi-throw overlay: 21 templates

Temporal coverage is a deterministic overlay on repetitions of the 1,184 base
cells, not another Cartesian product. The frozen IDs and behaviors are:

```text
SQ01 static preview then throw
SQ02 prelook with Q off then throw
SQ03 yaw adjustment then throw
SQ04 pitch adjustment then throw
SQ05 combined yaw/pitch adjustment then throw
SQ06 overshoot, reverse/correct, then throw
SQ07 cancel, look elsewhere, reacquire, then throw
SQ08 same object with different arcs
SQ09 same object from different approaches
SQ10 different objects consecutively
SQ11 object -> floor -> object
SQ12 object -> near miss -> wall
SQ13 floor bounce -> hoop -> open landing
SQ14 stationary multi-throw while retaining Q
SQ15 release Q, relocate, reacquire, then throw
SQ16 mixed immediate, short, and cooldown-long Q retention
SQ17 rejected cooldown E edge followed by valid recovery
SQ18 two-grenade persistence
SQ19 three-grenade persistence
SQ20 four-grenade persistence
SQ21 long random five-or-more-grenade persistence
```

Every template expands into explicit executable steps. Each accepted throw
records its template and step ID, associated base cell, preview and E frames,
camera state, temporal profiles, grenade ID, intended and realized contact
evidence, rest/tail evidence, semantic credit, and preview/flight parity.
Templates that require re-aiming require at least 2.5 degrees of camera delta;
the catalog also records a 0.015 minimum trajectory-image-change ratio for
future image-space reporting. Runtime validation rejects a nominally different
throw that does not satisfy its required measurable trajectory difference.

The overlay covers all aim-acquisition, post-throw camera, Q-retention, and
movement profiles. Later launch positions are reached only through recorded
actions; no template teleports the player, snaps the camera, tracks a grenade,
or removes an older grenade before episode reset.

## 7. Post-throw mission variation

Every mission cell receives progressive post-throw variation without multiplying
the mandatory discrete catalog:

- Q release: immediate, short retain, substantial cooldown retain
- movement after release: stationary, forward, backward, strafe left, strafe
  right, mirrored diagonal, ordinary semi-Markov
- camera after throw: preserve aim, continue a preselected camera hold, ordinary
  survey
- tail style: remain, pass by outcome area, move away, or continue traversal

These dimensions are assigned by stable recipe ordinal so every mission family
and target eventually receives every profile as X increases. No profile uses the
grenade location as a camera target.

